# XSH fsync()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# fsync() is the File Synchronization option (FSC). That data reached a
# storage device cannot be observed without a crash and is not tested
# (the page's RATIONALE says so too); the assertions check return values,
# errors, and that the call does not disturb the file. Error tests arrange
# exactly one error condition (XSH 2.3).

assertion  XSH.fsync.001
anchor     functions/fsync.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fsync.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       FSC
since      2001
says       fsync() of a descriptor open for writing on a regular file that
           was just written returns 0, and afterwards the file has the
           same length and contents and the descriptor the same file
           offset.
test       t/001-regular.c

assertion  XSH.fsync.002
anchor     functions/fsync.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      conditional
when       FSC
since      2001
says       fsync() of a descriptor that is not open, and of -1, fails with
           EBADF.
test       t/002-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fsync.003
anchor     functions/fsync.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fsync.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      disputed
when       FSC
since      2001
says       fsync() of the read end of a pipe either returns 0 or fails
           with EINVAL; any other outcome is a failure.
note       disputed (new: fsync-pipe): the ERRORS section requires
           EINVAL when the descriptor does not refer to a file on which
           the operation is possible, but nothing says which file types
           those are, and the RATIONALE permits a null implementation.
           Both outcomes are therefore accepted, and only another errno
           (or a hang) fails.
test       t/003-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.fsync.004
anchor     functions/fsync.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fsync.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      disputed
when       FSC
since      2001
says       fsync() of a descriptor open only for reading on a regular file
           returns 0.
note       disputed (new: fsync-rdonly): the page does not say whether a
           descriptor not open for writing is acceptable (fdatasync()'s
           page says outright that it is); the descriptor is valid and
           the operation is possible on a regular file, so the test
           takes the reading that it succeeds. A FAIL is logged as
           INSPECT.
test       t/004-rdonly.c

assertion  XSH.fsync.005
anchor     functions/fsync.html#APPLICATION USAGE
anchor     functions/fsync.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       FSC
since      2001
says       fsync() of a descriptor open on a directory either returns 0
           or fails with EINVAL; any other outcome is a failure.
note       The usage section says an application can call it on a
           directory; a file system on which that is not possible may
           report EINVAL.
test       t/005-directory.c

assertion  XSH.fsync.006
anchor     functions/fsync.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fsync.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       FSC
since      2001
says       Data written through one descriptor of a regular file, fsync()
           through a second descriptor opened separately on the same
           file returns 0 and the file keeps the data.
note       The description concerns the file named by the descriptor,
           not the descriptor that did the writing.
test       t/006-other-descriptor.c
